Choose one business problem from the following or from your domain:

Fraud detection
Customer churn
Credit scoring
Demand forecasting
Product recommendation
Employee turnover
Sales forecasting
Customer segmentation
Inventory management
Marketing campaign targeting
Answer: Marketing campaign targeting

What is the business problem?
Write 4-6 sentences.
Marketing campaign targeting follows the problem of how best to advertise the product to an audience. It needs a healthy balance between keeping returning/loyal customers and targeting newer audiences. The marketing needs to return a profit: if the money spent on advertisements or promo's is more than the resulting influx of customers and revenue, than the marketing campaign should be considered a failure. There are several avenues to track a targeted marketing campaign's success: revenue increase, customer increase, rating increase, reach, etc.

Why is this problem important to the organization?
Write 4-6 sentences.
This is an important problem because its solution directly benefits most aspects of the company. If a good, consistent campaign is run, then all facets of the company will increase, and allow for further reach. If a bad campaign is run, then time and money is wasted, resulting in a net-loss for the organization. Thus, the problem is important and should be taken seriously with lots of research and effort invested in generating a good campaign.

Who is affected by this problem?
Customers, sales team, finance department, marketing department. 

Part 2 — Identify the Data — 10 Points

Think about what information an AI system would need.

Complete:

What data would you need?
Ratings, reviews, size of customer base, price of product, and previous marketing strategies if possible.

Where could the data come from?
I will be using Google Play Store app data from Kaggle:
L. Gupta, "Google Play Store Apps," Feb 2019. [Online]. 
Available: https://www.kaggle.com/lava18/google-play-store-apps

How much historical data might be useful?
Previous marketing campaigns would be extremely useful to see what type of advertising works best on which audiences, and which generates the most revenue.

What would be the most important variables?
Ratings, size of customer base, and price of products.

What could be missing or inaccurate?
Some customers will not leave reviews on the product, so ratings and reviews can be a little inaccurate and biased. Certain individuals will spend far more money than the average depending on their disposition, and this information is impossible to predict with reasonable data. The data set I am using is specifically for Google Play, which is largely dominated by android users, and therefore the data cannot necessarily be generalized to all apps across multiple brands. Increases in app usage cannot be directly correlated to organization’s efforts, i.e. some external factor could result in sudden increases, like a popular content creator using it.

